      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm putting together a doc for our new CI/CD pipeline for the fintech project. Can you give me a quick breakdown of the main stages—specifically Code Commit, Automated Testing, Staging, and Production—and what kind of quality gates we should have at each step to make sure nothing breaks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, here's a breakdown of your CI/CD pipeline stages with recommended quality gates:\n\n1. **Code Commit:** Upon code check-in, block no direct deployment to production. Implement a Linter to enforce code standards and a Unit Test that must pass before proceeding to verify single-responsibility and basic logic.\n\n2. **Build:** Compile code to ensure that nothing is broken. Check for successful build and compatibility with the intended runtime environment. Use Docker or a similar technology for consistent environments.\n\n3. **Automated Testing:**\n    - *Unit Tests:* Run all unit tests to mask out each unit of the application independently.\n    - *Integration Tests:* Perform comprehensive tests on combined pieces to validate interactions.\n    - *End-to-End Tests (E2E):* Simulate real user scenarios to affirm that the complete flow works as expected.\n\n4. **Staging:** Confirm that deployment to this environment is entirely automatic after successful builds and tests. Conduct performance and security tests, along with the previously mentioned others, using real-world data simulations influencing the deployment to the staging area to increase confidence in the code.\n\n5. **Production:** Use blue-green deployments or canary releases to minimize downtime and ensure no impact on live users. Include robust analytics to",
      "source": "target",
